Poryg said:
In Javascript you can have 50 plugins extending the same function through aliasing. This allows you to have 50 plugins that can be compatible with each other as long as they aren't interfering with each other. In C# method overriding is impossible.
Click to expand...
Not quite. In C#, there's a thing called "virtual" and "abstract" functions. If you wanted to override a method, you would inherit from the same class and literally use the override keyword.
public class Animal
{
    public virtual void MakeASound()
    {
        throw new System.Exception("You didn't override me!");
    }
}

public class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void MakeASound()
    {
        Debug.Log("Bark bark!");
    }
}


public class GreatDane : Dog
{
    public override void MakeASound()
    {
        base.MakeASound();

        Debug.Log("WOOF WOOF!");
    }
}
Calling Animal.MakeASound() will throw an error, but it's made to be overridden.
Dog.MakeASound() will print "Bark bark" in the console. It's completely overridden the Animal.MakeASound().
GreatDane.MakeASound() will print both "Bark bark" AND "WOOF WOOF!". Calling base.MakeASound() will call the logic from Dog.MakeASound (the base class it inherits from).
You can recycle the same logic from a base class and extend it.
Tbh, I find this cleaner than JS plugins littering object definitions in 50 different places.
