I see we've got a syntax clash here. I'm a C++ developer, so I know how virtual and abstract functions work. In fact they work the same in Javascript too, although you don't see it much there. However, I am not talking about overriding parent definition with child's definition. But overriding the very function itself. In other words changing the definition of an already existing function.
Code:
function foo() {
console.log("foo");
   };
var bar = foo;
foo = function() {
   bar.call();
console.log("bar");
   };

foo();
Calling foo() will print "foo" and "bar".
It is a kind of dirty practice, however given the large community drive behind the plugins it offers a very easy way to integrate even potentially incompatible plugins. No such thing will be possible in Unity, because we won't be able to change function definitions to fix compatibility issues between plugins.

Oooo yeah, I see what you mean there.
Yikes, yeah that is really anti-OOP which C# is the foundation for.
Tbh, I'd be fired if I wrote anything like that at work lol.
In the end, to accomplish this in C#, you'd have to use inheritance or maybe Action and Delegates.
C#:
public class FooClass {
    public Action BeforeMethodCall;
    public Action AfterMethodCall;

    public void Foo() {
        BeforeMethodCall?.Invoke();

        Debug.Log("foo").

        AfterMethodCall?.Invoke();
    }
}

var fooObj = new FooClass();

fooObj.BeforeMethodCall += delegate() { Debug.Log("I happen BEFORE foo is printed.")};
fooObj.AfterMethodCall += delegate() { Debug.Log("I happen AFTER foo is printed.") };

fooObj.Foo();


// Console Output:
// I happen BEFORE foo is printed.
// foo
// I happen AFTER foo is printed.
Events are pretty handy for injecting logic.
For any JS devs, delegates are pretty much anonymous functions.

